 ## Overview
-Custom Pass Rendering is a deferred, multi-pass rendering architecture designed for high-performance visualization of large-scale volumetric data structures, specifically Octrees and Sparse Voxel DAGs (SV-DAGs) [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering complex, sparse 3D geometry by decoupling ray traversal, attribute computation, and shading into distinct, sequential GPU passes [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike single-pass ray marchers, this method first computes hit positions and normals for every screen pixel via hardware-accelerated ray traversal, storing them in intermediate framebuffers, which are then processed by specialized passes for occlusion, lighting, and material lookup [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from sibling pipelines by its explicit separation of the "hit" computation (position/normal) from the final shading, allowing for advanced post-processing effects like SSAO and bilateral filtering to be applied to the geometric data before final color composition [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Custom Pass Rendering is a multi-pass, object-order rendering pipeline designed for high-performance visualization of large-scale volumetric data (specifically NanoVDB) and octree-based geometry within a modern graphics API context [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently computing surface attributes (position, normal, material ID) and lighting effects (SSAO, shadows) for sparse volumetric datasets by decoupling geometric traversal from shading [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike monolithic ray-marching shaders, this method uses a sequence of specialized FullScreenPass stages to build intermediate G-buffers (hit position, filtered normals, ambient occlusion) before a final shading pass [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is distinct from [Plug-and-Play Ray Marching and Rendering](981-plug-and-play-ray-marching-and-rendering.md) by its explicit separation of traversal and lighting into discrete render passes, and from [Fragment List-Based Rendering Pipeline](3488-fragment-list-based-rendering-pipeline.md) by its reliance on screen-space fragment buffers rather than explicit primitive lists [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
+The pipeline executes a fixed sequence of render passes. Each pass renders a full-screen quad (or uses a compute-shader-equivalent full-screen dispatch) to process the entire screen resolution, reading from and writing to specific Frame Buffer Objects (FBOs).
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-**Inputs:**
-*   Camera parameters: View matrix, projection matrix, inverse view matrix, inverse projection matrix, camera position, screen resolution ($W \times H$), field of view (FOV = 60 degrees) [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   Volume data: An encoded Octree or SV-DAG structure stored in GPU buffers (e.g., `usamplerBuffer` or `usampler3D`) [S1, unofficial].
-*   Material data (optional): A texture or buffer containing material IDs and colors [S1, unofficial].
-*   Light parameters: Light position, light size [S1, unofficial].
-
-**Outputs:**
-*   Final color buffer: A screen-resolution RGBA texture containing the shaded image [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Inputs:** Camera matrices (View, Projection, Inverse View, Inverse Projection), screen resolution, volume data (NanoVDB octree or DAG), optional material octree, light position, and previous pass outputs.
+*   **Outputs:** Final color buffer with alpha blending.
 
 ### Data Structures and Layout
